    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d891">

                <pb facs="0943" n="931" xml:id="pg931"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.61/787: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-01-25T20:00:00+03:00">January 25, 1941—8
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received January 25—6:35 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>170. I have had a further talk this evening with Lozovski in the course of which
                    I again urged him to take action in the matter of the Soviet wives,<anchor
                        n="15" xml:id="d891-f1"/> the discrimination against the American newspaper
                    correspondents and the Roszkowski case. I also brought to his attention the
                    recent demand of the Soviet authorities that American citizens pay for their
                    railroad transportation from Moscow to Vladivostok in American currency (see my
                    144, January 23, 8 p.m.<anchor n="16" xml:id="d891-f2"/>). I further called to
                    his attention the large number of notes from the Embassy to the Commissariat of
                    Foreign Affairs during 1940 which remain unanswered. He stated that he would
                    give these matters his immediate attention.</p>
                <p>Lozovski referred to the lifting of the moral embargo<anchor n="17"
                        xml:id="d891-f3"/> as “merely the correction of a condition which never
                    should have existed,” and expressed the hope that the questions of the Baltic
                    ships, gold and Legations would be similarly “corrected” at an early
                        date.<anchor n="18" xml:id="d891-f4"/> In reply I expressed the hope that
                    full compensation would soon be offered by the Soviet Government for American
                    properties confiscated in the Soviet occupied areas.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d891-f1" n="15" xml:id="d891-f1-content">
                <p>For previous correspondence on this subject, see memorandum by the Ambassador in
                    the Soviet Union of March 14, 1938, and footnote 34, <ref
                        target="frus1933-39#pg_533">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, The Soviet Union, 1933–1939,
                        pp. 533</ref>–<ref target="frus1933-39#pg_536">536</ref>; and his despatch
                    No. 19, August 16, 1939, <ref target="frus1933-39#pg_844">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, p. 844</ref>. See also the undated
                    memorandum by the Assistant Chief of the Division of European Affairs, and
                    telegrams No. 1329, October 10, 1940, No. 1733, December 15, 1940, and No. 1776,
                    December 23, 1940, from the Ambassador in the Soviet Union, <ref
                        target="frus1940v03#pg_331">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1940, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">iii</hi>, pp. 331</ref>, <ref
                        target="frus1940v03#pg_393">393</ref>, <ref target="frus1940v03#pg_418"
                        >418</ref>, and <ref target="frus1940v03#pg_436">436</ref>,
                    respectively.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d891-f2" n="16" xml:id="d891-f2-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg868">p. 868</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d891-f3" n="17" xml:id="d891-f3-content">
                <p>See note of January 21 to the Ambassador of the Soviet Union, <ref
                        target="#pg696">p. 696</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d891-f4" n="18" xml:id="d891-f4-content">
                <p>For correspondence on difficulties affecting relations between the United States
                    and the Soviet Union, see <ref target="#pg667">pp. 667</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
